  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "fedora-rebuild should take two exclusive arguments, 1) list of directories and 2) list of pids. In the case of the list of directories, fedora-rebuild would rebuild in depth-first order.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "For example, fedora-rebuild.sh -d /opt/fedora/data/objectStore/2011/10 /opt/fedora/data/objectStore/2011/11 would recursively rebuild all of the objects found in those two directories.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Alternatively, fedora-rebuild.sh -p demo:123 demo:abc would rebuild the two objects specified.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Rationale: For a large repository, rebuild takes hours, if not days, during which system is unavailable. In a disaster-recovery scenario, we'd like to be able to get back online with at least the \"most important\" objects rebuilt as soon as possible.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "This also allows for selective recovery in the case of isolated inconsistencies that are detected.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Finally, this mechanism would allow for parallel rebuild processes to further speed the rebuild time.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
